The mechanism is as follows (pp. 163-171): —
1. Lower thick. The hindmost points of the pyramids (arytenoid cartilages) close together, an elliptical slit between the vocal ligaments (or chords), which vibrate through their whole length, breadth, and thickness fully, loosely, and visibly. The lid (epiglottis) is low.
2. Upper thick. "The elliptical chink disappears and becomes linear. The lid (epiglottis) rises; the vocal ligaments are stretched.
3. Lower thin. The lid (epiglottis) is more raised, so as to show the cushion below it, the whole larynx and the insertions of the vocal
ligaments in the shield (thyroid) cartilage. The vocal ligaments are quite still, and their vibrations are confined to the thin inner edges. The vocal ligaments are made thinurr and transparent, as shown by illumination from lielow. Male voices cease here.
between the vocal ligaments. When this is used by men it gives the falsetto arising from the upper thin being carried below its true place. This slit is gradually reduced in size as the contralto and soprano voices ascend. U,
4. Upper thin. An elliptical slit again forms
contracts for at least two-thirds of its length, the vocal ligaments being pressed together so tightly that scarcely any trace of a slit remains, and no vibrations are visible. The front part opens as an oval chink, and the edges of this vibrate so markedly that the outline is blurred. The drawings of the two lost registers (pp. 168169) were made from laryugoscopic examination of a lady.
5. Small. The back part of the glottis
